        </div>

        <div style="flex:1 1 240px;min-width:0;padding:20px;box-sizing:border-box;border:1px solid #5a76a3;border-radius:12px;background:#263245b3;">
          <div style="font-size:2rem;font-weight:700;line-height:1.2;margin-bottom:8px;">5%</div>
          <p style="margin:0;">said AI would improve people's ability to form meaningful relationships.</p>
        </div>

        <div style="flex:1 1 240px;min-width:0;padding:20px;box-sizing:border-box;border:1px solid #5a76a3;border-radius:12px;background:#263245b3;">
          <div style="font-size:2rem;font-weight:700;line-height:1.2;margin-bottom:8px;">≈⅔</div>
          <p style="margin:0;">said AI should play no role in judging whether two people could fall in love.</p>
        </div>

      </div>

      <p>These results do not mean that people reject AI in online communication. They show that there is a distinction between using AI as a practical tool and allowing AI to make decisions about deeply personal relationships.</p>

      <p>That distinction is important for random-chat platforms: AI can potentially help with moderation, accessibility, translation and recommendations without becoming the decision-maker for human relationships.</p>

      <p style="font-size:0.9rem;opacity:0.8;">
